      "text": "Mathematically, let's assume Jane's current age is \\( x \\).\n\nOne year ago, Jane's age was \\( x - 1 \\).\nOne year from now, Jane's age will be \\( x + 1 \\).\n\nGiven that \\( x - 1 \\) is a perfect square and \\( x + 1 \\) is a perfect cube, we need to find the value of \\( x \\) that satisfies these conditions. Let's start by listing some perfect squares and perfect cubes close to Jane's current age \\( x \\):\n\nPerfect squares close to Jane's age: \\( 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100 \\)\nPerfect cubes close to Jane's age: \\( 1, 8, 27, 64 \\)\n\nWe need \\( x - 1 \\) to be one of these perfect squares, and \\( x + 1 \\) to be one of these perfect cubes. Let's check each perfect square:\n\n1. If \\( x - 1 = 4 \\), then \\( x = 5 \\). Check the cube: \\( 5 + 1 = 6 \\), which is not a perfect cube. So, \\( x \\neq 5 \\).\n2. If \\( x - 1 = 9 \\), then \\( x = 10 \\). Check the cube: \\( 10 + 1 = 11 \\), which is not a perfect cube. So, \\( x \\neq 10 \\).\n3. If \\( x - 1 = 16 \\), then \\( x = 17 \\). Check the cube: \\( 17 + 1 = 18 \\), which is not a perfect cube. So, \\( x \\neq 17 \\).\n4. If \\( x - 1 = 25 \\), then \\( x = 26 \\). Check the cube: \\( 26 + 1 = 27 \\), which is a perfect cube (\\( 3^3 = 27 \\)). So, \\( x = 26 \\) is a candidate.\n5. If \\( x - 1 = 36 \\), then \\( x = 37 \\). Check the cube: \\( 37 + 1 = 38 \\), which is not a perfect cube. So, \\( x \\neq 37 \\).\n6. If \\( x - 1 = 49 \\), then \\( x = 50 \\). Check the cube: \\( 50 + 1 = 51 \\), which is not a perfect cube. So, \\( x \\neq 50 \\).\n7. If \\( x - 1 = 64 \\), then \\( x = 65 \\). Check the cube: \\( 65 + 1 = 66 \\), which is not a perfect cube. So, \\( x \\neq 65 \\).\n\nFrom the above checks, the only value of \\( x \\) that satisfies both conditions is \\( x = 26 \\).\n\nTherefore, Jane is \\(\\boxed{26}\\) years old.",
